    "narrative": "The heart of our lineup today rests on two elite-tier bats facing soft matchups: **Corbin Carroll** (80.77) and **Josh Naylor** (72.66). **Corbin Carroll** earns his top billing by exploiting a high-upside matchup against Jose Quintana; despite a slight platoon dip, his elite sprint speed and favorable xwOBA make him a primary engine for our SB and R production. Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** is positioned perfectly at the #3 spot against Seth Lugo. The 4.44 xERA of the opposing starter provides a clean runway for **Josh Naylor** to drive in runs, supported by a strong +5% platoon adjustment. **James Wood** (71.89) rounds out our offensive core, benefiting from a +10% order bonus as the leadoff man against a middling Mart\u00edn P\u00e9rez, cementing his status as a high-floor, high-ceiling play.\n\nStrategic platoon advantages and park factors are heavily influencing our secondary selections. **Zach McKinstry** (57.64) has surged into the starting lineup thanks to a massive +16% park adjustment, making him a high-efficiency play despite the lower base projection. On the riskier side, **Brice Turang** (47.97) remains in the lineup for his SB potential, but he faces a stern test against Yoshinobu Yamamoto; the -17% SP skill penalty and the pitcher's elite pickoff efficiency (58% SB suppression) significantly cap his realistic ceiling today. Our most difficult bench decision involved **Kyle Karros** (44.96), who narrowly missed the cut. While he possesses a decent base, the combination of a #9 batting order slot and a neutral matchup against Ryne Nelson left him just shy of the efficiency required to usurp our current starters. All managers should note that these scores remain contingent on confirmed lineups; **Dalton Rushing** and **Gabriel Moreno** in particular carry slight risk depending on whether their assumed batting positions materialize as finalized.",
